[{:db/id -1
  :adr/id "ADR-2608190300"
  :adr/title "A slow model, an unreachable provider and a bug in this application are three problems"
  :adr/status "accepted"
  :adr/date "2026-08-19"
  :adr/body
  "## Context

With the plan-contract deadlock closed (ADR-2608190200), the largest remaining
resident failure was a timeout: 7 of 24 runs on 2026-08-19. Every one was
recorded as `:internal-error`, which is also what a genuine bug in this
application produces. Finding out that they were timeouts meant reading the
event log of each run by hand and noticing that the message happened to say
`request timed out`.

`java.net.http` throws `HttpTimeoutException` with no ex-data, so
`(:type (ex-data error))` was nil and the catch-all applied. Nothing was
broken; nothing distinguished either.

## Decision

**Three failures, three names.** `timeout->typed` classifies at the two
request sites:

| | |
|---|---|
| `HttpConnectTimeoutException` | `:provider/unreachable` — configuration or network |
| `HttpTimeoutException` | `:provider/timeout` — capacity |
| anything else | rethrown untouched |

Order is load-bearing: `HttpConnectTimeoutException` **extends**
`HttpTimeoutException`, so the obvious `instance?` check in the wrong order
reports an unreachable provider as a slow model — the same conflation this
change exists to undo, one layer down.

**A timeout is not a no-op.** `complete-resident-no-op!` admits
`#{:provider/empty-response :provider/http-error :blocked}`: all three mean the
provider ANSWERED and had nothing to add, so the host's own receipts settle
what happened. A timeout means the tick never found out, and recording it as a
completed no-op would claim the Bot looked and saw nothing.

The admitted set lives **in** that function rather than at its call sites. The
first version of this change put the check in one caller and left the function
willing to complete anything; the test for `:provider/timeout` is what found
it. A call site that forgets is a call site that completes a run it should
have failed.

**A person is told it ran out of time**, with the limit. `実行に失敗しました`
is what every unclassified failure says, and it gives someone nothing to act
on.

## What the numbers say, and what they do not settle

Measured against `murakumo-main` under live load, 2026-08-19: **11.4 output
tokens/second, 75.8 prompt tokens/second, one production slot**
(`parallel: 1`). A resident tick is given 768 output tokens — 68 seconds — and
carries roughly 3.3k prompt tokens per turn, another 44. **That is 112 seconds
of a 120 second wall**, so a tick that waits even briefly for the slot exceeds
it. Median time from `run/started` to first model output was 35s across 24
runs; the timeouts are the tail — 126s, 137s, 208s, 261s.

`request-timeout-seconds` is now a named var carrying that arithmetic, rather
than a literal `120` written twice.

**The number is not changed here.** Raising it is not obviously right: with one
slot, a request allowed to run longer holds the whole workforce longer.
Lowering it frees the slot sooner but fails work that would have finished. The
cheaper direction is a smaller prompt, which is a change to what a resident
tick is asked, not to how long it may take. That decision needs an operator,
and now it has numbers.

## Verification

Four changes, each reverted alone, each reddening only its own tests:

  not typing the timeout          -> a-request-that-runs-out-of-time-is-a-provider-timeout,
                                     the-classifier-keeps-three-failures-apart
  the subclass order              -> the-classifier-keeps-three-failures-apart
  the admitted-reason set         -> a-resident-timeout-is-not-laundered-into-a-safe-no-op
  the timeout's own message       -> a-timeout-tells-the-person-it-ran-out-of-time

The classifier is tested directly rather than through a socket, because the
condition cannot be produced reliably from here: a `com.sun.net.httpserver`
that has been stopped does not refuse the connection on this platform, it
hangs, and the JDK reports a plain `HttpTimeoutException`. That was measured
while trying to write the socket version, and it is why the first draft of
that test asserted something the environment could not deliver."}]
